{"author":"Joe Oakhart","author_email":"joeoakhart@navapbc.com","data":{"body":"FIRST 4-ARM ADJUDICATION - ts-carbon, Fleiss kappa 0.856, Krippendorff alpha 0.857, zero contested items across 64 ballot rows and 89 relation candidates. Per DISTINCT ISSUE, which is the comparable unit: ce 23 tp of 27 distinct with 0 fp; sp 11 of 15 with 0 fp; mini 11 of 12 with 1 fp; rebar 8 of 10 with 0 fp. REBAR IS THE ONLY ARM THAT OVERSTATES ITS OUTPUT: its 15 rows collapse to 10 distinct issues, five same-arm merges, a 50 percent row inflation, while ce sp and mini all net NEGATIVE inflation because judges split their rows into more issues than claimed. This reproduces the Wave 1 duplication signature - one issue filed under several criteria, the only arm that does it - on a clean unscaffolded concurrently-run plan where every arm read identical bytes, so the behaviour is not an artifact of input asymmetry or of scaffolding. Consequence for reporting: per-row counts flatter rebar by half. The per-row figure on this run is 13 tp of 15; the honest per-distinct-issue figure is 8 of 10. Zero false positives either way, which is consistent with rebar's precision profile across the study."},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1789447075608594001,"uuid":"273e85ba-5ccc-4ca0-8573-1f70ee0b2110"}